IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lrmp-dates`

-- Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) -- the questions of a claimant.
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing the claim. Mostly that is the person pursuing, for a dead person's estate, a claim the dead
-- person had (the executor or administrator, or the family member acting for the estate): "Does my late mother's claim
-- survive her death?", "Can her estate still recover damages for her pain and suffering from her asbestos (or silica)
-- disease?", "Can the estate claim exemplary damages, lost earnings for the years she would have lived, funeral
-- expenses?". It is also the injured person who has a claim against someone who has since died ("Can I still claim
-- against his estate?"), the injured person or the dependants whose lump-sum damages for future loss are being worked
-- out ("What discount rate applies?", s 5), and a married person (s 2, s 3). The courts, the Governor and the Attorney
-- General get no seat.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The Act imposes no duty with a deadline, so there is no regulative rule.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole operative Act: s 2 (unity of spouses abolished), s 3 (spousal capacity), s 4 (effect of
-- death on causes of action: (1A), (1), (2)(a)-(e), (2a), (3), (4), (5)), s 5 (discount rate for future loss). Out:
-- s 1 (short title and commencement), s 5(2) (the Governor's power to fix a rate by Order: machinery; its result is
-- asked). Reasons in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: the compilation on the Western Australian Legislation website retrieved 7 September 2026, last amended by the
-- Civil Liability Amendment (Provisional Damages for Dust Diseases) Act 2024 Part 3 (in force 1 December 2024),
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47978.txt. No Order fixing a discount rate under s 5(1)(d) is
-- deposited: whether one is in force, and its rate, are asked.
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments are INPUTS. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of
-- 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words, NOTHING where it does not
-- answer (or the question does not arise).

§ `Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941: the questions of a claimant`

-- The kinds of cause of action the Act names. The proviso to s 4(1) names three that do not survive; s 4(2)(b) names
-- breach of promise to marry. Every other cause of action (a personal injury claim, a dust disease claim, a contract
-- claim) is the last choice.
DECLARE `Kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `defamation`
    `seduction`
    `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other`
    `breach of promise to marry`
    `any other cause of action (for example personal injury or a dust disease)`

GIVEN k IS A `Kind of claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `excepted by the proviso to s 4(1)` k IF
        k EQUALS `defamation`
    OR  k EQUALS `seduction`
    OR  k EQUALS `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other`

§§ `s 4(1) -- does the claim survive the death`

-- s 4(1): "Subject to the provisions of this section and the Limitation Act 2005, on the death of any person after the
-- commencement of this Act all causes of action subsisting against or vested in him shall survive against, or, as the
-- case may be, for the benefit of, his estate. Provided that this subsection shall not apply to causes of action for
-- defamation or seduction or for inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other."
--
-- The order of the answers:
--   1. a death on or before 24 October 1942: outside the subsection's scope ("after the commencement"), no "only":
--      NOTHING (ruling (c) of 2026-11-05);
--   2. a cause of action that accrued before 15 November 2005: the Limitation Legislation Amendment and Repeal Act 2005
--      s 15(4) keeps s 4 as it stood before that day for it, and that earlier text is not deposited: NOTHING
--      (finding 4);
--   3. defamation, seduction, inducing a spouse to leave: the proviso's flat negative, JUST FALSE;
--   4. a claim already out of time under the Limitation Act 2005: "subject to ... the Limitation Act 2005" points
--      outside the sources: NOTHING (fork F1);
--   5. otherwise JUST TRUE.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE, `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE, `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim`,
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`date of death` `falls after` `the commencement of this Act`) THEN NOTHING
        IF `the 2005 limitation commencement day` `falls after` `date the cause of action accrued` THEN NOTHING
        IF `excepted by the proviso to s 4(1)` `the kind of claim` THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `out of time` THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export Does the claim of a person who has died survive for the benefit of their estate (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(1))? Causes of action vested in a person who dies survive for their estate, except defamation, seduction and inducing a spouse to leave or stay apart. JUST TRUE yes; JUST FALSE the Act says no; NOTHING the deposited Act does not answer (death before 24 Oct 1942, a cause of action that accrued before 15 Nov 2005, or a claim already out of time under the Limitation Act 2005).
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued (for an injury, usually the date the injury or disease was suffered or became known; this is a question for the Limitation Act 2005)
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for: defamation; seduction; inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other; breach of promise to marry; or any other cause of action, such as personal injury or a dust disease
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim has already run out under the Limitation Act 2005 (answer no if unsure or if proceedings were brought in time)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1) -- does the claim survive for the estate` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time`

@export Can I still claim against the estate of the person who wronged me, now that they have died (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(1), (4))? Causes of action against a person who dies survive against their estate, except defamation, seduction and inducing a spouse to leave or stay apart; and if they died before (or at the same moment as) you suffered the damage, you are treated as having had the claim against them before they died (s 4(4)). JUST TRUE yes; JUST FALSE the Act says no; NOTHING the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `their date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person you are claiming against died
      `date your cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date your cause of action accrued (for an injury, usually the date you suffered it; if they died before you suffered the damage, the date you suffered it)
      `your kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is for: defamation; seduction; inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other; breach of promise to marry; or any other cause of action, such as personal injury
      `your claim out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing your claim has already run out under the Limitation Act 2005 (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1), (4) -- can I claim against the estate of the person who died` `their date of death` `date your cause of action accrued` `your kind of claim` `your claim out of time` MEANS
    `survives the death under s 4(1)` `their date of death` `date your cause of action accrued` `your kind of claim` `your claim out of time`

-- s 4(4): "Where damage has been suffered by reason of any act or omission in respect of which a cause of action would
-- have subsisted against any person if that person had not died before or at the same time as the damage was suffered,
-- there shall be deemed ... to have been subsisting against him before his death such cause of action ..." A "where"
-- condition: when it fails, JUST FALSE (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). A death after the damage needs no deeming: the
-- cause of action subsisted anyway, and the answer to this narrower question is JUST FALSE.
@export If the person whose act or omission harmed me died before (or at the same moment as) I suffered the damage, am I treated as having had a claim against them before they died (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(4))? JUST TRUE yes, so the claim can survive against their estate under s 4(1); JUST FALSE s 4(4) does not apply (for example, they died after the damage, when the claim existed anyway).
GIVEN `would have had a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would have had a claim against them for that act or omission had they still been alive when you suffered the damage
      `died before or with the damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they died before, or at the same time as, you suffered the damage
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(4) -- is a claim deemed to have existed against the person before they died` `would have had a claim` `died before or with the damage` MEANS
    IF `would have had a claim` AND `died before or with the damage`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 4(2), (2a), (3) -- what the estate can recover`

-- Every question here arises only where the cause of action survives for the estate ("Where a cause of action survives
-- as aforesaid for the benefit of the estate"). Where s 4(1) does not give JUST TRUE, these questions answer NOTHING:
-- the question does not arise, or the deposited Act does not answer it.

-- s 4(2a), (3): the dust-disease exceptions to s 4(2)(d).
-- (2a): (a) death after the day the Asbestos Diseases Act 2002 came into operation (21 March 2002), (b) the death results
-- from a latent injury attributable to the inhalation of asbestos caused by the act or omission giving rise to the cause
-- of action, (c) proceedings instituted by the person before death and pending at death.
-- (3): (a) death after the day Part 3 of the Dust Diseases Act 2024 came into operation (1 December 2024), (b) the death
-- results from a latent injury that resulted from the inhalation of silica caused by the act or omission, (c) the person
-- had instituted proceedings pending at death.
-- "proceedings" includes an initial action and a subsequent action under the Civil Liability Act 2002 Part 2 Division 5
-- (s 4(1A)); borrowed, so asked as a fact.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE, `death from a latent injury` IS A BOOLEAN, `asbestos` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `silica` IS A BOOLEAN, `proceedings instituted and pending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the asbestos exception in s 4(2a) applies` IF
        `date of death` `falls after` `the commencement of the Asbestos Diseases Act 2002`
    AND `death from a latent injury`
    AND `asbestos`
    AND `proceedings instituted and pending`

GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE, `death from a latent injury` IS A BOOLEAN, `asbestos` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `silica` IS A BOOLEAN, `proceedings instituted and pending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the silica exception in s 4(3) applies` IF
        `date of death` `falls after` `the commencement of Part 3 of the Dust Diseases Act 2024`
    AND `death from a latent injury`
    AND `silica`
    AND `proceedings instituted and pending`

@export Does the law stop my late relative's estate recovering damages for their pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm, or shortened life (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(d), (2a), (3))? The estate cannot recover these damages, unless the person died of a latent asbestos disease (death after 21 Mar 2002) or a latent silica disease (death after 1 Dec 2024) caused by the wrongdoing and had brought proceedings that were still pending when they died. JUST TRUE the Act stops it; JUST FALSE the bar does not apply; NOTHING the claim does not survive or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued (for a dust disease, usually when the disease was suffered or became known)
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for; a personal injury or dust disease claim is "any other cause of action"
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005 (answer no if proceedings were brought in time)
      `death from a latent injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death resulted from a latent injury (a disease or injury that stays hidden for years, such as mesothelioma, asbestosis or silicosis) caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `asbestos` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that latent injury is attributable to breathing in asbestos, which the act or omission the claim is about caused
      `silica` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that latent injury resulted from breathing in silica, which the act or omission the claim is about caused
      `proceedings instituted and pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had started court proceedings on the claim (including an initial or subsequent action under the Civil Liability Act 2002 Part 2 Division 5) before they died, and those proceedings were still pending when they died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(d) -- does the law stop the estate recovering damages for pain and suffering` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` `death from a latent injury` `asbestos` `silica` `proceedings instituted and pending` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN IF     `the asbestos exception in s 4(2a) applies` `date of death` `death from a latent injury` `asbestos` `silica` `proceedings instituted and pending`
                OR  `the silica exception in s 4(3) applies` `date of death` `death from a latent injury` `asbestos` `silica` `proceedings instituted and pending`
                THEN JUST FALSE
                ELSE JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING

@export Does the law stop my late relative's estate recovering exemplary (punitive) damages (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(a))? The damages recoverable for the estate never include exemplary damages. JUST TRUE the Act stops it; NOTHING the claim does not survive or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(a) -- does the law stop the estate recovering exemplary damages` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING

@export Does the law stop my late relative's estate recovering their lost earnings, or loss of earning capacity, for the years after their death that they would have lived but for the wrongdoing (the "lost years") (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(e))? JUST TRUE the Act stops it; NOTHING the claim does not survive or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(e) -- does the law stop the estate recovering earnings for the lost years` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 4(2)(b): "in the case of a breach of promise to marry shall be limited to such damage, if any, to the estate of that
-- person as flows from the breach of promise to marry". Fork F2: "in the case of" read as a "where" condition, so another
-- kind of claim gets JUST FALSE.
@export Is the estate's claim for a breach of promise to marry limited to the damage the breach did to the estate (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(b))? JUST TRUE yes; JUST FALSE the limit does not apply because the claim is of another kind; NOTHING the claim does not survive or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(b) -- is the estate's claim for breach of promise to marry limited to damage to the estate` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN IF `the kind of claim` EQUALS `breach of promise to marry`
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 4(2)(c): "where the death of that person has been caused by the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of
-- action, shall be calculated without reference to any loss or gain to his estate consequent on his death, except that
-- a sum in respect of funeral expenses may be included". A "where" condition: when the death was not caused by the act
-- or omission, JUST FALSE (ruling (b)).
@export If the wrongdoing caused the death, are losses and gains to the estate that follow from the death (such as insurance paid out, or an annuity that ends) left out of the estate's damages (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(c))? JUST TRUE they are left out (funeral expenses may still be included); JUST FALSE the rule does not apply because the wrongdoing did not cause the death; NOTHING the claim does not survive or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
      `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(c) -- are gains and losses from the death left out of the estate's damages` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` `death caused by the wrong` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN IF `death caused by the wrong`
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 4(2)(c), the exception: "a sum in respect of funeral expenses may be included". Fork F3: where the death was not
-- caused by the act or omission, the paragraph is silent about funeral expenses: NOTHING (not JUST FALSE).
@export Can the estate's damages include a sum for funeral expenses, where the wrongdoing caused the death (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(2)(c))? JUST TRUE they may be included; NOTHING the Act does not say (the death was not caused by the wrongdoing, or the claim does not survive).
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
      `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(c) -- can the estate's damages include funeral expenses` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` `death caused by the wrong` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN IF `death caused by the wrong`
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE NOTHING
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 4(5) -- the estate's claim and the dependants' claims`

-- s 4(5): "The rights conferred by this Act for the benefit of the estates of deceased persons shall be in addition to
-- and not in derogation of any rights conferred on the dependants of deceased persons by the Imperial Act 9th and 10th
-- Victoria, Chapter 93 ...". A flat negative ("not in derogation"): JUST FALSE. Fork F4: the Imperial Act (Lord
-- Campbell's Act) was repealed for Western Australia by the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 s 2 (note 1 of the compilation);
-- the encoding reads the reference as reaching the dependants' claim that replaced it (finding 3).
@export Does the estate's surviving claim cut down the family's own claim as dependants for the death (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 4(5))? No: the estate's rights are in addition to, and not in derogation of, the dependants' rights. JUST FALSE it does not cut it down; NOTHING the estate has no surviving claim or the deposited Act does not answer.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person whose claim it was died
      `date the cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The date their cause of action accrued
      `the kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What the claim was for
      `out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time for bringing the claim had already run out under the Limitation Act 2005
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(5) -- does the estate's claim cut down the dependants' claim` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` MEANS
    IF `survives the death under s 4(1)` `date of death` `date the cause of action accrued` `the kind of claim` `out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 5 -- the discount rate for future loss`

-- s 5(1): "Where an award of damages that relates to personal injury or the death of a person is to include
-- compensation, awarded as a lump sum, in respect of damage for future loss that is referable to (a) loss or impairment
-- of capacity to earn; (b) loss or diminution of future probable earnings; or (c) a liability to incur expenditure in
-- the future, the present value of the future loss shall be quantified by adopting (d) a discount rate of the
-- percentage fixed by the Governor by Order; or (e) where no percentage is fixed ..., a discount rate of 6%."
-- s 5(3): "personal injury includes any disease and any impairment of a person's physical or mental condition". An
-- "includes" definition: an injury, a disease or an impairment counts; the ordinary meaning is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Kind of future loss` IS ONE OF
    `loss or impairment of capacity to earn`
    `loss or diminution of future probable earnings`
    `a liability to incur expenditure in the future`
    `another kind of future loss`

GIVEN `an injury` IS A BOOLEAN, `a disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `an impairment` IS A BOOLEAN, `a death` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `relates to personal injury or death` IF
        `an injury` OR `a disease` OR `an impairment` OR `a death`

GIVEN `an injury` IS A BOOLEAN, `a disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `an impairment` IS A BOOLEAN, `a death` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `lump sum` IS A BOOLEAN, `the loss` IS A `Kind of future loss`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5(1) reaches this part of the award` IF
        `relates to personal injury or death` `an injury` `a disease` `an impairment` `a death`
    AND `lump sum`
    AND (NOT (`the loss` EQUALS `another kind of future loss`))

-- A "where" condition: when it fails, JUST FALSE (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05).
@export Does the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 5 fix the discount rate used to turn my future loss into a lump sum today? It does for lump-sum damages for personal injury (including disease and physical or mental impairment) or death, for future loss of earning capacity, of future earnings, or of future expenses. JUST TRUE yes; JUST FALSE no, s 5 does not reach this part of the award.
GIVEN `an injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to a bodily injury to a person
      `a disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to a disease
      `an impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to an impairment of a person's physical or mental condition
      `a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to the death of a person (for example a dependants' claim)
      `lump sum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the compensation for the future loss is to be awarded as a lump sum
      `the loss` IS A `Kind of future loss` @desc What the future loss is: loss or impairment of capacity to earn; loss or diminution of future probable earnings; a liability to incur expenditure in the future (such as future care or medical costs); or another kind of future loss
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) -- does s 5 fix the discount rate for this part of my award` `an injury` `a disease` `an impairment` `a death` `lump sum` `the loss` MEANS
    IF `s 5(1) reaches this part of the award` `an injury` `a disease` `an impairment` `a death` `lump sum` `the loss`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- The rate is a percentage, returned as a number of per cent (6 for 6%). Where s 5(1) does not reach the award there is
-- no rate: NOTHING (a rate cannot be a "no"; the "no" is the question above).
@export What discount rate, in per cent a year, must be used to work out the present value of my lump-sum damages for future loss (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 5(1))? The rate fixed by the Governor by Order, or 6% where no Order fixes one. NOTHING where s 5 does not reach this part of the award.
GIVEN `an injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to a bodily injury to a person
      `a disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to a disease
      `an impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to an impairment of a person's physical or mental condition
      `a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the award relates to the death of a person
      `lump sum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the compensation for the future loss is to be awarded as a lump sum
      `the loss` IS A `Kind of future loss` @desc What the future loss is: loss or impairment of capacity to earn; loss or diminution of future probable earnings; a liability to incur expenditure in the future; or another kind of future loss
      `an Order fixes a rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Order of the Governor under s 5(2), made on the Attorney General's recommendation, fixing a discount rate is in force (answer no if none has been made)
      `the rate fixed by Order` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage the Order fixes, in per cent (for example 3 for 3%); ignored if no Order fixes a rate
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 5(1) -- what discount rate applies to my future loss` `an injury` `a disease` `an impairment` `a death` `lump sum` `the loss` `an Order fixes a rate` `the rate fixed by Order` MEANS
    IF `s 5(1) reaches this part of the award` `an injury` `a disease` `an impairment` `a death` `lump sum` `the loss`
        THEN IF `an Order fixes a rate`
                THEN JUST `the rate fixed by Order`
                ELSE JUST 6
        ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 2, s 3 -- spouses`

-- s 2: "The common law doctrine of unity of spouses is abolished." s 3(1): a married person has a legal personality
-- independent, separate and distinct from the spouse's; s 3(2): the same legal capacity as if unmarried; s 3(3): whether
-- married before or after the section commenced. For a person who is not married the questions do not arise: NOTHING.
@export Is my legal personality separate from my spouse's, so that I am a person in law in my own right (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 2, s 3(1), (3))? JUST TRUE yes; NOTHING if you are not married (the question does not arise).
GIVEN `married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are married (whenever you married)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) -- is my legal personality separate from my spouse's` `married` MEANS
    IF `married` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

@export Does being married take away any legal capacity I would have if I were unmarried, for example to own property, make contracts, sue or be sued (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 (WA) s 2, s 3(2), (3))? JUST FALSE no: a married person has the same legal capacity as if unmarried; NOTHING if you are not married.
GIVEN `married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are married (whenever you married)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) -- does being married take away any of my legal capacity` `married` MEANS
    IF `married` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING
